speechtext:
That it be resolved by the House of Commons:
_ That a message be sent to the senate, informing Their Honours that this house has adopted the above resolution and requesting that Their Honours will unite with this house in the approval of the above mentioned convention and that the Clerk do carry the said message to the senate;
And that this house do approve of same.

I have grave doubts as to the constitutionality of this legislation, which affects the inspection of roots and vegetables. Clearly it deals with property and civil rights. Whether or not the provisions of section 91 of the British North America Act conferring upon parliament power to deal with matters of agriculture, is to be construed so widely as to enable it to invade entirely the field of property and civil rights in the provinces, I am not prepared at the moment to say. But I think it is my duty to direct the attention of the minister and the law officers of the crown to the provisions of the statute. Clause 3 reads;
No person shall sell, offer, expose, or have in possession for sale potatoes, onions, turnips, artichokes, beets, carrots, parsnips or such other vegetables as may be named in the act or by the governor in council under the provisions of this act unless such vegetables comply with all provisions of this act and regulations made thereunder.
That is a very sweeping section. When the bill was first introduced I took occasion to look into the law very roughly and I certainly think the Minister of Justice (Mr. Lapointe), when he looks into it, will have some hesitancy in proposing that this parliament, because it has jurisdiction over agiculture, coordinate jurisdiction with the provinces, has power thus to destroy the whole law with respect to property and civil rights. I am merely directing attention to the matter at the moment without going into it with any degree of care. It is certainly a serious matter to say that no person shall have in his possession any vegetable, onions, potatoes, carrots, unless he has complied with the provisions of this federal statute. It will be recalled that the courts of the country with respect to egg-grading legislation held that it was ultra vires of this parliament, and I think the application of the same principle to this legislation would indicate that it was invalid. I am not in any sense discussing the merits of the measure beyond directing attention to the matter as it struck me when I read the legislation in question.
